      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Fractional exhaled nitric oxide (FE(NO)) and exhaled carbon monoxide (ECO) have been proposed as markers of airway inflammation and oxidative stress."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "The aim of this study was to assess the effect of oral prednisone treatment on FE(NO) and ECO levels in a group of 30 asthmatic children with asthma exacerbation."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Thirty asthmatic children with asthma exacerbation were treated with oral prednisone for 5 days (1 mg/kg/day). Before and after prednisone therapy, ECO was measured by means of a chemical analyzer and FE(NO) was measured by means of a chemiluminescence analyzer. ECO and FE(NO) were also measured in a group of healthy nonatopic children."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Before therapy, both ECO values and FE(NO) values were higher in asthmatic children (ECO, 3.2 +/- 0.2 ppm; FE(NO) online, 74.9 +/- 6.2 ppb; FE(NO) offline, 20.2 +/- 1.4 ppb) than in healthy controls (ECO, 2.0 +/- 0.2 ppm [P <.01]; FE(NO) online, 10.1 +/- 0.8 [P <.0001]; FE(NO) offline, 5.9 +/- 0.4 ppb [P <.0001]). An overlap in ECO values was found between healthy controls and asthmatic children. After prednisone therapy, there was a significant reduction in FE(NO) values (FE(NO) online, 40.6 +/- 4.6 ppb [P <.0001]; FE(NO) offline, 11.1 +/- 0.8 ppb [P < 0.0001]) and a slight but nonsignificant decrease in ECO values (2.7 +/- 0.2 ppm [P = not significant]) in the asthmatic group. No significant correlation between ECO values and FE(NO) values was found in either the asthmatic children or the controls."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "After a course of prednisone therapy, in children with asthma exacerbation there is a significant decrease in FE(NO) but no significant change in ECO levels. This possibly suggests that ECO is less sensitive than FE(NO) to inhibition by corticosteroids."
        }
      ]},
